---
description: Require every runtime feature to remain bounded and correct in streamed large worlds
alwaysApply: true
---

# Large-world compatibility

- Treat large-world behavior as a design constraint for every runtime feature, not as a later optimization pass.
- Do not allocate, iterate, simulate, or render in proportion to the full world area when the feature can be bounded by the active chunk set, AOI, camera/player focus, or a fixed-size sliding window.
- Keep CPU work, GPU memory, draw calls, temporary allocations, and replicated state bounded as the configured world size grows. State the bound in code comments when it is not obvious.
- Preserve deterministic world placement and authoritative gameplay data. Visual streaming, LOD, pooling, and local effect windows must not change generated positions, server-owned state, or cross-backend world results.
- Handle chunk boundaries, negative coordinates, world edges, fast travel/teleport, focus changes, late async completion, and load/unload races explicitly. A large focus jump must reset or reproject local state safely instead of dragging stale state across the world.
- Release chunk-local and window-local geometry, materials, render targets, listeners, caches, and pending work when content leaves the keep radius or the scene is replaced.
- Scene-level Components must obey the same bounds: never hide full-world scans or allocations inside lifecycle hooks, and always release scene-owned listeners and local state when deactivated or disposed.
- Prevent sampling or collision data from bleeding outside its valid local region. Content outside a local simulation window must use a defined neutral/fallback state.
- Prefer chunk-local data or focus-following windows for dense visual effects. Use full-world textures, grids, scans, or caches only when their worst-case memory and update cost have been justified and tested.
- Verify new features in both fixed scenes and streamed-world paths where applicable. Add focused coverage for recentering, chunk load/unload, large coordinate jumps, bounded resource counts, and preservation of deterministic positions.
- A feature is not complete merely because it works near the origin or in a small test scene; it must remain usable after prolonged traversal through the streamed world.
